WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.615
Welcome to Module 7 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:06.537
extend Lifecycle Workflows.

3
00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:11.779
This module serves Gates 7 and 8 of the
implementation guide, and takes Chapter

4
00:00:11.779 --> 00:00:16.699
05 of Building the Attribute-Driven
Migration, on custom task extensions.

5
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:21.768
It comes after Modules 4 to 6
because the path follows the gates:

6
00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:25.862
Chapter 05 serves Gates 7
and 8, after the Gate 5 work.

7
00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:30.563
You'll see what Learn's Logic App
definition costs a GCC High leaver, and run

8
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:35.726
the definition that fixes it, through
transient failures and repeat deliveries.

9
00:00:35.726 --> 00:00:39.138
You'll build the deployment
pieces from a reviewed registry,

10
00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:42.550
and check that the leaver blocks
before the custom task runs.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.750
Nothing signs in.

12
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.622
The code is illustrative: tested
against fixtures and fakes,

13
00:00:47.622 --> 00:00:50.080
not against a production tenant.

14
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:54.980
Why does this matter?

15
00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.463
In this workbook's implementation guide,

16
00:00:57.463 --> 00:01:02.574
Gate 7 proves that one personnel action
changes access only through the attributes,

17
00:01:02.574 --> 00:01:05.568
with the mover workflow's
run history as evidence.

18
00:01:05.568 --> 00:01:09.096
Gate 8 puts leaver automation
first in every wave,

19
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:12.367
and sizes Lifecycle
Workflows for the agency.

20
00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.630
The built-in tasks cover the account.

21
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.468
The rest of a leaver, such as the PIV
credential, the devices and the servers,

22
00:01:19.468 --> 00:01:23.355
goes through a custom task
extension that calls a Logic App.

23
00:01:23.355 --> 00:01:24.543
And here's the gap.

24
00:01:24.543 --> 00:01:29.549
The definition on Microsoft Learn's
page, Configure a Logic App for Lifecycle

25
00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:32.600
Workflow use, calls back
to the worldwide Graph,

26
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.104
on beta, and it always says it completed.

27
00:01:35.104 --> 00:01:39.380
In a GCC High tenant that
callback goes to the wrong cloud.

28
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.068
And a revocation that
failed reads as done.

29
00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:47.120
In this module you see what that costs,
and you run the definition that fixes it.

30
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.681
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

31
00:01:53.681 --> 00:01:56.045
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

32
00:01:56.045 --> 00:01:59.553
You'll describe where custom
task extensions sit in a leaver,

33
00:01:59.553 --> 00:02:03.832
and the three rules of this workbook's
guidance that the kit builds in code.

34
00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:07.345
You'll explain what Learn's Logic
App definition leaves to you.

35
00:02:07.345 --> 00:02:11.038
You'll run a definition for a
cloud, through transient failures

36
00:02:11.038 --> 00:02:14.945
and repeat deliveries, and read
what Lifecycle Workflows records.

37
00:02:14.945 --> 00:02:18.020
You'll build the deployment
pieces from the registry,

38
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.095
and explain why an Azure
Government build is refused.

39
00:02:21.095 --> 00:02:24.008
And you'll check a workflow
read-back for the leaver order.

40
00:02:24.008 --> 00:02:28.836
Before you start, finish Module 1:
provisioning creates and updates accounts,

41
00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:32.608
and Lifecycle Workflows act on
accounts that already exist.

42
00:02:32.608 --> 00:02:38.500
Lab 1 needs Python 3.10 or later
and a POSIX shell, and no tenant.

43
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.950
This workbook's guidance splits the
work: inbound provisioning creates and

44
00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.800
updates accounts, and Lifecycle Workflows
act on accounts that already exist.

45
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.550
It also sets three rules, and
the kit builds them in code.

46
00:02:55.550 --> 00:03:00.188
One parameterized Logic App per
integration, not one per component.

47
00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:03.426
Block first: Disable user
account and Revoke all

48
00:03:03.426 --> 00:03:06.826
refresh tokens for user
run before the custom task.

49
00:03:06.826 --> 00:03:10.937
And custom tasks are idempotent,
and retry with a bound.

50
00:03:10.937 --> 00:03:13.288
Microsoft Learn sets the contract.

51
00:03:13.288 --> 00:03:18.489
Its page on the Lifecycle Workflows custom
task extension says that in launch and

52
00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:21.626
wait, the extension starts
the Logic App and waits;

53
00:03:21.626 --> 00:03:25.987
if no response arrives within the
wait, the task is considered failed.

54
00:03:25.987 --> 00:03:29.926
Learn's Graph reference sets the wait
at thirty minutes to three hours.

55
00:03:29.926 --> 00:03:32.435
The response is a POST to resume,

56
00:03:32.435 --> 00:03:37.263
with a status of completed or
failed, and Learn lists it in v1.0.

57
00:03:37.263 --> 00:03:42.953
And Learn's page on Microsoft Graph national
cloud deployments gives the clouds:

58
00:03:42.953 --> 00:03:47.624
GCC keeps the worldwide Graph, GCC
High and DoD have their own hosts,

59
00:03:47.624 --> 00:03:51.310
and a token for one cloud
doesn't work in another.

60
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.707
Microsoft Learn's page, Configure a
Logic App for Lifecycle Workflow use,

61
00:03:59.707 --> 00:04:02.573
gives the definition to
paste into code view.

62
00:04:02.573 --> 00:04:05.757
It's Microsoft's only sample
for this integration,

63
00:04:05.757 --> 00:04:09.797
so the repository pins the page,
from the entra docs repository.

64
00:04:09.797 --> 00:04:13.247
Running the definition against
fakes shows what it leaves to you.

65
00:04:13.247 --> 00:04:16.327
It resumes the task with
the Logic App's managed

66
00:04:16.327 --> 00:04:19.248
identity and no secret,
and the kit keeps that.

67
00:04:19.248 --> 00:04:23.461
But it hard-codes the worldwide
Graph host, and calls back on beta.

68
00:04:23.461 --> 00:04:25.929
It uses the callback path as it arrives,

69
00:04:25.929 --> 00:04:30.635
so any path appended to the Graph host
gets a POST with the identity's token.

70
00:04:30.635 --> 00:04:35.060
It always reports completed, so a
failed revocation reads as done.

71
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.549
If work you add before the callback fails,

72
00:04:37.549 --> 00:04:41.135
no callback is sent, and the task
waits out the whole timeout.

73
00:04:41.135 --> 00:04:42.873
It has no idempotency.

74
00:04:42.873 --> 00:04:46.709
And it carries the subject's
name and email into run history.

75
00:04:46.709 --> 00:04:49.704
The verdict: the right
shape and the right trigger,

76
00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:53.560
but not safe to run as the
leaver's revocation as published.

77
00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.948
The kit's definition is the
one every integration deploys.

78
00:05:00.948 --> 00:05:05.010
What changes between integrations
is data: four parameters,

79
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:09.723
the Graph host, the downstream system,
its audience, and the operations.

80
00:05:09.723 --> 00:05:11.598
A run goes in four steps.

81
00:05:11.598 --> 00:05:15.695
Check request checks that the callback
path is a resume path for this

82
00:05:15.695 --> 00:05:19.936
workflow and this task processing
result, and that the subject has an I D.

83
00:05:19.936 --> 00:05:24.885
If not, the run stops and sends nothing,
because it can't trust the path.

84
00:05:24.885 --> 00:05:29.398
Operation looks up the workflow; an
unregistered one gets no work done.

85
00:05:29.398 --> 00:05:34.265
Call integration posts to the downstream
system with an idempotency key,

86
00:05:34.265 --> 00:05:36.260
the task processing result I D.

87
00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:37.896
The key is the kit's design,

88
00:05:37.896 --> 00:05:41.948
a contract with the downstream
system, not a Microsoft feature.

89
00:05:41.948 --> 00:05:45.008
It retries transient
errors up to four times,

90
00:05:45.008 --> 00:05:50.136
with a retry policy from Learn's page
on handling errors in Azure Logic Apps.

91
00:05:50.136 --> 00:05:54.331
And Resume posts to the Graph
host on v1.0, after success,

92
00:05:54.331 --> 00:05:59.647
failure or time-out, and reports
completed only if the call succeeded.

93
00:05:59.647 --> 00:06:02.568
The registry holds one
entry per integration,

94
00:06:02.568 --> 00:06:07.860
and the builder refuses values outside
the ranges in Learn's Graph reference.

95
00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.672
The kit's tool has five commands.

96
00:06:13.672 --> 00:06:17.685
Lint applies twelve rules to any
definition, Learn's included.

97
00:06:17.685 --> 00:06:21.431
One of them, L X eleven, adds
up the worst-case retry waits

98
00:06:21.431 --> 00:06:25.760
and fails the definition if they
reach the extension's timeout.

99
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.758
Run runs a definition
against fakes for a cloud,

100
00:06:28.758 --> 00:06:31.910
with downstream failures
and repeat deliveries.

101
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:36.253
Build writes the Logic App's ARM
resource and the extension's body,

102
00:06:36.253 --> 00:06:38.585
and refuses if lint finds anything.

103
00:06:38.585 --> 00:06:42.248
And two checks compare what you read
back from Graph with the registry.

104
00:06:42.248 --> 00:06:47.120
For authorization, the builder writes the
proof-of-possession policy from Learn's

105
00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.490
page, Configure a Logic App for Lifecycle
Workflow use, with shared access

106
00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:52.851
signatures turned off,

107
00:06:52.851 --> 00:06:56.648
as Learn's best practices for
securing the custom extension ask.

108
00:06:56.648 --> 00:06:59.976
Learn documents that policy
only for Azure commercial,

109
00:06:59.976 --> 00:07:04.535
so for a Logic App in Azure Government
the builder refuses rather than guess.

110
00:07:04.535 --> 00:07:07.699
The callback identity needs
no tenant-wide Lifecycle

111
00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:10.723
Workflows permission, which
Learn advises against.

112
00:07:10.723 --> 00:07:16.211
And the workflow check requires the leaver
to block first, which is this workbook's

113
00:07:16.211 --> 00:07:21.340
guidance, in Microsoft's order: disable,
then revoke, then the custom task.

114
00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:25.815
Try it.

115
00:07:25.815 --> 00:07:31.315
Lab 1 runs in the lifecycle extensions folder
of the examples, and nothing signs in.

116
00:07:31.315 --> 00:07:34.931
Step one extracts Learn's
definition from the pinned page,

117
00:07:34.931 --> 00:07:37.115
and lints it with a one-hour timeout.

118
00:07:37.115 --> 00:07:39.528
Eight results, and exit code two.

119
00:07:39.528 --> 00:07:44.490
Five are findings: it hard-codes the
worldwide Graph host, calls back on beta,

120
00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.371
uses the callback path
without checking it,

121
00:07:47.371 --> 00:07:51.853
sends no callback when the work
fails, and always reports completed.

122
00:07:51.853 --> 00:07:56.552
Two warnings are for run history, and
one for the implicit retry policy.

123
00:07:56.552 --> 00:08:00.914
Step two runs Learn's definition
for a GCC High tenant.

124
00:08:00.914 --> 00:08:05.363
The one POST goes to the worldwide
Graph, on beta, and the last line

125
00:08:05.363 --> 00:08:10.315
is what Lifecycle Workflows records:
failed, no callback within an hour.

126
00:08:10.315 --> 00:08:14.540
So a GCC High leaver's task
fails at the end of the wait.

127
00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:18.640
Run it again for a worldwide
tenant, and the task is completed.

128
00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.040
Step three lints the program's definition.

129
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.765
With a one-hour timeout: no findings.

130
00:08:27.765 --> 00:08:32.795
With a fifteen-minute timeout, L X eleven
finds that the worst-case retry waits,

131
00:08:32.795 --> 00:08:34.839
nine hundred and sixty seconds,

132
00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:38.140
reach the callback timeout,
and the exit code is two.

133
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.852
So the retries have to
fit inside the wait.

134
00:08:40.852 --> 00:08:45.777
Step four runs the program's
definition for GCC High, three ways.

135
00:08:45.777 --> 00:08:49.395
Through two transient failures:
three downstream calls,

136
00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:51.703
one effect, and the task is completed.

137
00:08:51.703 --> 00:08:54.988
Through five failures:
five calls, no effect,

138
00:08:54.988 --> 00:08:59.003
and the task is failed, at once,
not at the end of the wait.

139
00:08:59.003 --> 00:09:01.802
And with two deliveries
of the same request:

140
00:09:01.802 --> 00:09:04.990
two calls, but one effect,
and the task is completed.

141
00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:08.653
The idempotency key means
the work is done once.

142
00:09:08.653 --> 00:09:14.415
Every callback goes to the
GCC High Graph host, on v1.0.

143
00:09:14.415 --> 00:09:17.840
The slide joins each run's last two lines.

144
00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.853
Step five builds the deployment
pieces from the registry,

145
00:09:24.853 --> 00:09:27.640
for the card management
revocation integration.

146
00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:32.640
It writes two files: the Logic App's
ARM resource, and the extension's body.

147
00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.653
Then set the Logic App's cloud to Azure
Government, build again, and put it back.

148
00:09:37.653 --> 00:09:42.021
The second build exits one: Learn
documents the proof-of-possession

149
00:09:42.021 --> 00:09:45.453
policy only for Azure
commercial, so nothing is built.

150
00:09:45.453 --> 00:09:47.628
Step six checks the read-backs.

151
00:09:47.628 --> 00:09:52.015
The extension and the leaver workflow
both come back with no findings.

152
00:09:52.015 --> 00:09:54.446
Then move the custom task to the front of

153
00:09:54.446 --> 00:09:56.877
a copy of the workflow,
and check the copy.

154
00:09:56.877 --> 00:10:00.554
Two W X oh three findings:
Disable user account,

155
00:10:00.554 --> 00:10:05.490
and Revoke all refresh tokens,
don't run before the custom task.

156
00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:06.603
Block first.

157
00:10:06.603 --> 00:10:07.852
Exit code two.

158
00:10:07.852 --> 00:10:11.365
Step seven runs the tests: fifty-five, OK.

159
00:10:11.365 --> 00:10:13.100
Step eight cleans up.

160
00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:21.240
Lab 2 runs a real leaver, in a test
tenant with Microsoft Entra ID

161
00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:26.137
Governance licenses and a test Azure
subscription, never production.

162
00:10:26.137 --> 00:10:29.399
You need the Lifecycle
Workflows Administrator role,

163
00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:33.601
and Logic App Contributor, Contributor
or Owner on the resource group.

164
00:10:33.601 --> 00:10:37.021
Create the extension and its
Logic App in the admin center,

165
00:10:37.021 --> 00:10:41.012
record what the admin center made,
and check it against the registry.

166
00:10:41.012 --> 00:10:43.574
Replace the definition with the program's.

167
00:10:43.574 --> 00:10:47.471
Build an on-demand test leaver
workflow that blocks first,

168
00:10:47.471 --> 00:10:49.663
and run it for one fictional user.

169
00:10:49.663 --> 00:10:51.349
Then drill the failure path:

170
00:10:51.349 --> 00:10:55.162
point the integration at a receiver
that answers five-oh-three,

171
00:10:55.162 --> 00:10:59.562
and confirm the task ends failed within
minutes, not at the end of the hour.

172
00:10:59.562 --> 00:11:04.432
To back it out, remove the custom task
and the workflow, then the extension,

173
00:11:04.432 --> 00:11:09.301
the Logic App, the resource group, the
role assignments and the test users.

174
00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:11.820
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

175
00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:16.957
The federal requirement.

176
00:11:16.957 --> 00:11:21.772
A custom task extension carries the part
of a leaver the built-in tasks don't,

177
00:11:21.772 --> 00:11:25.033
so its evidence is account
and credential removal.

178
00:11:25.033 --> 00:11:30.333
A callback that reports what really happened,
at once, with one effect per delivery,

179
00:11:30.333 --> 00:11:35.401
supports NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three controls A C two,

180
00:11:35.401 --> 00:11:38.987
account management, and P S
four, personnel termination;

181
00:11:38.987 --> 00:11:41.794
the evidence is the
task processing result.

182
00:11:41.794 --> 00:11:45.544
The definition passing lint,
with long retries refused,

183
00:11:45.544 --> 00:11:47.907
leaves the pull request's check run.

184
00:11:47.907 --> 00:11:52.371
Building from the reviewed registry,
with shared access signatures off and

185
00:11:52.371 --> 00:11:56.621
proof-of-possession on, leaves the
repository and the deployment record.

186
00:11:56.621 --> 00:12:01.270
The read-back checks, and the leaver
blocking first, are filed with the change.

187
00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.707
The extension's authorized apps show the

188
00:12:03.707 --> 00:12:06.645
callback identity has no
tenant-wide permission.

189
00:12:06.645 --> 00:12:11.283
And in Lab 2, an on-demand run
leaves the Logic App's run history.

190
00:12:11.283 --> 00:12:14.895
The KSI column is FedRAMP's
Key Security Indicators,

191
00:12:14.895 --> 00:12:18.171
the FedRAMP 20x indicators for providers.

192
00:12:18.171 --> 00:12:22.080
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

193
00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.805
Check yourself with two questions.

194
00:12:27.805 --> 00:12:32.280
First: Learn's definition
runs for a GCC High leaver.

195
00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.543
What does Lifecycle Workflows record?

196
00:12:34.543 --> 00:12:37.338
The answer is b: failed,
at the end of the wait,

197
00:12:37.338 --> 00:12:41.092
because the callback went to
the worldwide Graph on beta.

198
00:12:41.092 --> 00:12:45.204
You saw it in step two: failed,
no callback within an hour.

199
00:12:45.204 --> 00:12:49.393
Second: why does the builder refuse
a Logic App in Azure Government?

200
00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:54.263
The answer is b: Learn documents the
proof-of-possession policy only for Azure

201
00:12:54.263 --> 00:12:58.905
commercial, so the builder refuses
rather than guess, and nothing is built.

202
00:12:58.905 --> 00:13:00.568
You saw that in step five.

203
00:13:00.568 --> 00:13:05.108
The module page has two more
questions, on five downstream failures

204
00:13:05.108 --> 00:13:09.450
and on W X oh three, each linked
to the section that answers it.

205
00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:14.025
To sum up.

206
00:13:14.025 --> 00:13:17.680
You ran a custom task extension's
definition for the right cloud,

207
00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:22.024
through failures and repeat deliveries,
built its deployment pieces from a

208
00:13:22.024 --> 00:13:26.713
reviewed registry, and checked that the
leaver blocks before the custom task runs.

209
00:13:26.713 --> 00:13:28.629
The evidence an ISSO keeps:

210
00:13:28.629 --> 00:13:33.595
the registry and the built ARM and
extension files in the repository,

211
00:13:33.595 --> 00:13:38.822
the check output filed with the change,
and, from Lab 2, the task processing

212
00:13:38.822 --> 00:13:44.049
results, the Logic App run history,
the drill record and the activity logs.

213
00:13:44.049 --> 00:13:46.946
Gate 7's own evidence,
the mover workflow's

214
00:13:46.946 --> 00:13:50.013
run history joined on
the key, is in the guide.

215
00:13:50.013 --> 00:13:53.712
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 05 checklist.

216
00:13:53.712 --> 00:13:58.600
Next is Module 8: run the drift
pipeline and hand over the evidence.

217
00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:03.390
You'll run the validation kit on
a schedule with no stored secret,

218
00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:09.240
compare each run with the last, and hand
the ISSO a package sha256sum verifies.
